                <h2 class="accordion-header">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                            type="button"
                            data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                            data-bs-target="#varQuestionOne">
                        What is var in Java?
                    </button>
                </h2>

                <div id="varQuestionOne"
                     class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                     data-bs-parent="#varQuestions">

                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        <code>var</code> is a local variable type-inference feature
                        introduced in Java 10. The compiler determines the variable's
                        type from its initializer.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                            type="button"
                            data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                            data-bs-target="#varQuestionTwo">
                        Is Java dynamically typed because of var?
                    </button>
                </h2>

                <div id="varQuestionTwo"
                     class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                     data-bs-parent="#varQuestions">

                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        No. Java remains statically typed. The variable type is
                        inferred at compile time.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                            type="button"
                            data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                            data-bs-target="#varQuestionThree">
                        Can var be used for fields?
                    </button>
                </h2>

                <div id="varQuestionThree"
                     class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                     data-bs-parent="#varQuestions">

                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        No. Java's <code>var</code> is intended for local variable
                        declarations, not class fields.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                            type="button"
                            data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                            data-bs-target="#varQuestionFour">
                        Can var be declared without initialization?
                    </button>
                </h2>

                <div id="varQuestionFour"
                     class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                     data-bs-parent="#varQuestions">

                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        No. The compiler needs an initializer to infer the variable type.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

            <div class="accordion-item">
                <h2 class="accordion-header">
                    <button class="accordion-button collapsed"
                            type="button"
                            data-bs-toggle="collapse"
                            data-bs-target="#varQuestionFive">
                        Can var be used with lambda expressions?
                    </button>
                </h2>

                <div id="varQuestionFive"
                     class="accordion-collapse collapse"
                     data-bs-parent="#varQuestions">

                    <div class="accordion-body">
                        A bare <code>var</code> declaration cannot infer the target
                        functional-interface type of a lambda expression.
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>

        </div>

